  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/metynnis-fasciatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Most Metynnis species become around 15 cm long.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/metynnis-fasciatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Metynnis fasciatus was described on the basis of a young fish from Brazil (Rio Capiru). The type specimen is regarded as lost.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/metynnis-fasciatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>All Metynnis feed on plant material.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/metynnis-fasciatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Males and females can be told apart by the shape of the anal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/metynnis-fasciatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>This beautiful fish, a serrasalmid and thus a relative of piranhas and pacus, is still enigmatic for scientists. The species is identical with M. hypsauchen in all respects except the coloration in young specimens. In M. fasciatus a pattern of regular and bold stripes exists which is usually lacking in M. hypsauchen. Until now no adult specimens with a regular stripe pattern were known to scientists, and it was thought that M. fasciatus represents only a rare juvenile phase of M. hypsauchen.</p><p>However, comparatively large (8-10 cm) specimens of this extraordinary silver dollar from Brazil have been imported again. Each specimen shows an individual pattern, but all were already sexually differentiated and thus definitely no longer juveniles.</p><p><strong>Lexicon:</strong> Metynnis: ancient Greek, means “in between the share” (the share being the ploughshare, the cutting blade of a plough); this refers to the spine before the dorsal fin. fasciatus: means “striped”.</p><p><strong>Common name:</strong> Striped silver dollar</p>"
    }
  ],
